NetSSL_Win: do not clobber the peer host name before the handshake

SecureSocketImpl::stateClientConnected() unconditionally overwrote _peerHostName
with the peer's IP address, discarding the name HTTPSClientSession had already
supplied via setPeerHostName(). InitializeSecurityContextW then received that IP
as the SNI server name, so hosts served from a name based virtual host answered
with the wrong certificate or with 421 Misdirected Request.

Guard the assignment the way stateClientHandshakeCondition() and
stateServerHandshakeCondition() in the same file already do, so the peer address
is only a fallback for when no name is known.

diff --git a/NetSSL_Win/src/SecureSocketImpl.cpp b/NetSSL_Win/src/SecureSocketImpl.cpp
index c6d38d8..39d3557 100644
--- a/NetSSL_Win/src/SecureSocketImpl.cpp
+++ b/NetSSL_Win/src/SecureSocketImpl.cpp
@@ -811,7 +811,8 @@ void SecureSocketImpl::stateError()
 
 void SecureSocketImpl::stateClientConnected()
 {
-	_peerHostName = _pSocket->peerAddress().host().toString();
+	if (_peerHostName.empty())
+		_peerHostName = _pSocket->peerAddress().host().toString();
 	setState(ST_CLIENT_HSK_START);
 }
 
